@section Additional options for this module

This module provides no additional options.

@section General

This backend accepts 78K/III instructions in the mnemonic and operand
notation used by NEC's documentation. It has been used with the standard
syntax module only (the assembler is built as @code{vasm78k3_std}).

The instruction set is not coded by hand in the backend: the mnemonic
table is generated by @code{gen_mnemonics.c} from the same opcode table
(@code{nec78k3_opcodes.h}) that is used by an independent disassembler,
so that code disassembled with that tool assembles back to identical
bytes.

The target address type is 16 bit and the byte order is little-endian.
Instructions consist of one up to four bytes (more when a branch has been
relaxed, see below) and require no alignment.
There is also no alignment requirement for sections and data.

There are no mnemonic qualifiers. Word-sized operations are separate
mnemonics (@code{movw}, @code{addw}, @code{subw}, ...).

@section Operand syntax

@table @code
    @item a, x, b, c, d, e, h, l
    @itemx ax, bc, de, hl, sp, psw, pswl, pswh, cy
        Registers, register pairs and flags. The alternate-bank register
        names written with a trailing apostrophe (for example @code{x'},
        @code{ax'}) are accepted.
    @item #expr
        Immediate value.
    @item !expr
        Absolute 16-bit address.
    @item expr
        A short-direct (@code{saddr}, FE20H-FF1FH) or special-function
        register (@code{sfr}, FF00H-FFFFH) address, or a branch target,
        depending on the instruction. For the @code{saddr} and @code{sfr}
        forms the address must be resolvable when the instruction is
        assembled (for example a symbol defined with @code{.equ}).
    @item sfr[expr]
    @itemx sfr[expr].w
        A special function register (byte or word access). The value may
        be given as the full address or as its low byte.
    @item [de+] [de-] [hl+] [hl-] [de] [hl] [vp] [up]
        Register indirect addressing.
    @item [de+a] [hl+a] [de+b] [hl+b] [vp+de] [vp+hl]
        Based indexed addressing.
    @item [de+n] [sp+n] [hl+n] [up+n] [vp+n]
        Based addressing with a byte offset.
    @item [expr+de] [expr+a] [expr+hl] [expr+b]
        Indexed addressing with a 16-bit base address.
    @item @{ax,bc,de,hl@}
        Register list of @code{push}, @code{pop}, @code{pushu} and
        @code{popu}. The registers @code{ax}, @code{bc}, @code{vp},
        @code{up} (@code{psw} for @code{pushu}/@code{popu}), @code{de} and
        @code{hl} may be listed in any order.
@end table

Bit instructions (@code{set1}, @code{clr1}, @code{not1}, @code{bt},
@code{bf}, @code{btclr}, @code{bfset}, @code{mov1}, @code{and1},
@code{or1}, @code{xor1}) take the bit number in NEC's notation, as part of
the byte operand it belongs to:

@example
        set1    flags.3
        bt      sfr[TMIC0].7,label
        mov1    cy,flags.3
        and1    cy,/flags.3        ; inverted bit
@end example

The bit number may be any constant expression and is checked to be in
the range 0 to 7. The form with the bit as a separate operand
(@code{set1 flags,3}, @code{bt flags,label,3}) and the form attaching the
bit to @code{cy} (@code{mov1 cy.3,flags}, @code{andi1 cy.3,flags},
@code{ori1 cy.3,flags}) are accepted as well. A name that contains a dot
and is already defined is taken as a name, not as @code{byte.bit}.

@section Extensions

This backend provides the following specific extensions:

@itemize @minus

@item @code{br} chooses between the short relative form and the absolute
 form according to the distance to the target. Conditional branches whose target
 is out of the short relative range are assembled as the inverted branch
 over an absolute @code{br}, and @code{dbnz} is relaxed in the same way.

@item The standard syntax module treats the apostrophe of the alternate
 register names (@code{ax'}) as part of the operand, as it does for the Z80.

@end itemize

@section Known Problems

    Some known problems of this module at the moment:

@itemize @minus

@item @code{sel rbn,alt} is not supported; @code{sel rbn} is.

@item Only the standard syntax module has been used with this backend.

@end itemize

@section Error Messages

This module has the following error messages:

@itemize @minus
@item 2001: illegal operand
@item 2002: value out of range
@item 2003: data size not supported
@item 2004: this addressing mode is not yet implemented in the 78k3 backend
@item 2005: relative branch target out of range (must fit in a signed 8-bit displacement)
@item 2006: saddr/saddrp operand 0x%x out of range (must be FE20H-FF1FH)
@item 2007: sfr/sfrp operand 0x%x out of range (must be FF00H-FFFFH)
@item 2008: addr11 (CALLF target) 0x%x out of range (must be 0800H-0FFFH)
@item 2009: addr5 (bit-address literal) 0x%x out of range (must be 00H-1FH)
@item 2010: saddr operand 0x%x is outside FE20H-FF1FH
@item 2011: based-addressing offset 0x%x out of range (must be 00H-FFH)
@item 2012: bit number 0x%x out of range (must be 0-7)
@item 2013: bit number missing (write it as byte.bit, or as a separate operand)
@item 2014: bit number given twice (as byte.bit and as a separate operand)
@end itemize
